>> source: https://www.gimmesomeoven.com/instant-pot-crispy-carnitas/

# Pressure cook

:mix @{pork shoulder|3 lb|cut into 2 in pieces}, @{kosher salt|1 tbsp}, @{black pepper|1 tsp}, @{ground cumin|1 tsp}, @{cinnamon|1/2 tsp}, @{five spice|1/4 tsp}, @{dried oregano|1/2 tsp}, @{bay leaf|2|torn}, @{onion|1|medium, diced}, @{?hot pepper|2|jalapeno, ancho, or other, chopped}, @{garlic|8 cloves|or more}, @{orange|1|navel, zested and halved} zest in #{instant pot} until evenly mixed => _pork.
:squeeze the halved orange over _pork.
  (the orange itself adds too much bitterness)
:{cook|30 min} _pork in #{instant pot|lid sealed, high pressure}, then natural release.
:remove _pork from #{instant pot}, discarding the garlic and bay leaves; save the oil and juices => _carnitas.
  (for a big batch, freeze portions with some cooking liquid)

# Crisp and serve

:crisp _carnitas either spread on #{sheet pan}, lightly crushed to release the meat strands, and broiled, or tossed with the juices in #{cast iron pan} => _{crisp carnitas}.
:serve _{crisp carnitas} however you like.

>> note: Leftover carnitas oil and juices make great fried rice flavoring.
>> note: Make pickled red onions for a side.
>> note: Adding a beer made it more watery than I like.
>> note: With a bone-in pork shoulder, save the bone for soup or broth, or toss it into the instant pot for extra flavor.
